import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const outlierCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'outlier', label: 'fifth value (the outlier)', min: 8, max: 1000, step: 1, value: 10, unit: 'k' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'mean of {2, 4, 6, 8, x}', expr: '(2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + outlier)/5' },
    { label: 'median (frozen at 6)', expr: '6' },
    { label: 'mean − median gap', expr: 'abs((2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + outlier)/5 - 6)' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/foundational/describing-data">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A dataset of a million numbers is unreadable; statistics compresses it into a few honest
    summaries. A <strong>centre</strong> (mean, median or mode), a <strong>spread</strong>
    (range, standard deviation, interquartile range), and a <strong>shape</strong> (the
    histogram). The first skill is knowing which summary to trust — and they disagree most
    exactly when it matters.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four calm values and one loud stranger — drag the outlier and watch which summary flinches" widget="sliderLab" config={outlierCfg} />

  <p>
    The mean chases the outlier; the median sits still. That single drag is why "average income"
    (mean, inflated by a few enormous fortunes) and "median income" (the middle person) tell
    different stories about the same country — and why news reports that swap them quietly are
    editing the story.
  </p>

  <h2>The tools, honestly named</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mean</strong> — balances the data like a seesaw; best for well-behaved, symmetric data.</li>
    <li><strong>Median</strong> — the middle rank; immune to outliers, ideal for incomes, house prices, waiting times.</li>
    <li><strong>Mode</strong> — the most frequent value; the only one that works on categories (most common blood type).</li>
    <li><strong>Standard deviation</strong> — the typical distance from the mean. For bell-shaped data the rule is <strong>68–95–99.7</strong>: about 68% of values fall within 1 SD, 95% within 2, 99.7% within 3.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Two classic traps</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Simpson's paradox:</strong> aggregated numbers can reverse the story in every
    subgroup — the University of California, Berkeley (1973) appeared biased against women
    overall while most individual departments favoured them, because women applied to the most
    competitive programmes. <strong>Misleading axes:</strong> a bar chart starting at 90 instead
    of 0 can make a 1% difference look like a landslide. Always ask what the axis is hiding.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bickel, Hammel & O'Connell, Science (1975) on Berkeley admissions; OpenStax, Introductory Statistics.</p>
</TopicPage>
